---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How this works"
  description="How this site records who owns a manufacturer, where the work happens, and when a shop is heavily automated."
>
  <p class="crumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a>
    <span aria-hidden="true"> / </span>
    <span>How this works</span>
  </p>
  <div class="page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>How this works</h1>
    <p class="lede">A file on each manufacturer. Two facts, kept apart.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="two-lines">
    <h2 id="two-lines">The two lines</h2>
    <div class="questions" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">01</p>
        <h3>Who owns and runs it</h3>
        <p>
          Who owns the company, and who runs it day to day. A plant in the United States with a foreign parent stays on the foreign-parent side of this line. The factory address does not change the owner.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="question">
        <p class="kicker">02</p>
        <h3>Where it is made</h3>
        <p>
          Where the manufacturing happens. A company owned in the United States and producing abroad is recorded as not made in the USA, or as split when the work really is in more than one place.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="filed">
    <h2 id="filed">What gets filed</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Each company is a JSON file in this site’s repository. The page is that file. Profiles are not kept in Netlify Blobs.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The page stays on the manufacturer: owners, plant, dated changes, sources. Patterns and prices stay on the company’s own site.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        A made-in-USA directory can put a name on a lead list. It does not fill this one, and this site does not copy that directory’s filters or product grids.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        If a document is missing, or two accounts disagree, the profile says so. A fact that was not read is not written up as established.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="automation">
    <h2 id="automation">When a shop is heavily automated</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Some companies are American-founded, American-owned, and American-run, and the plant is also heavily automated. When the sources support that, the file can include a short note: a label, a sentence or two, and the source ids.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The note is a fact about the shop. It is not a warning. It does not move the company down the list or off it. The list on the front page is alphabetical.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        If the sources do not establish how automated the plant is, leave the field off. An empty field means not established. It does not mean someone verified a fully manual shop, and the file should not store <span class="path">heavilyAutomated: false</span>.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        Set the note only when the ownership line is already American-owned and American-run, and the write-up supports an American founding. A foreign-owned plant does not get this note. Using some machines is not enough. The sources have to support calling the shop heavily automated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="shelves">
    <h2 id="shelves">Shelves, then the file</h2>
    <div class="stack" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p class="prose">
        Categories are how a shopper browses: flatware now, and another shelf when a company is filed under it. The category page names the goods and the manufacturer. The profile is still the company, with the change log and the sources.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        The number on a card is explained on <a href="/how-we-rate">How we rate</a>. Three lines are scored. Automation is the fourth line, shown, and worth zero.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p style="margin-top:2rem">
    <a href="/">Back to the list</a>
  </p>
</Base>
